Finn crafted his finest work—a vessel with a lid that sealed without gap or breath. The gardener stored her precious seeds and locked them away.

Years passed and she finally opened the jar. Every seed had died, suffocated in its perfect preservation. What she meant to protect, she had destroyed through protection itself.

In despair, she scattered the dead seeds outside. Seasons turned. Where she'd thrown them, new plants emerged—carried there by birds who'd found the scattered seeds, softened by rain, awakened by their exposure to earth and air.

She returned to Finn. "Your perfect vessel became a tomb," she said.

Finn held a dead seed gently. "True preservation isn't about sealing things away unchanged. Seeds need exchange with the world—moisture, cold, warmth, even decay—to become what they're meant to be. Sometimes protecting something from all change means preventing it from living at all."

